.TH awk "" "Command"
.SH NAME
awk \- report generation, pattern scanning and processing language
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBawk [\-y] [\-F\fIc\fB] [\-f \fIprogfile\fB] [\fIprog\fB] [\fIfile ...\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
awk -- report generation, pattern scanning and processing language
Usage:	awk [ -y ] [ -Fc ] [ -f progfile ] [ prog ] [ file ... ]
Options:
	-y	Dual case match (as in grep)
	-Fc	Set field separator character to 'c' (default: blank and tab)
	-f	'progfile' is the awk program; otherwise,
		the first non-option argument ('prog') is the awk program
If no 'file' is present, the standard input is used.
File '-' means the standard input.
.HE
.B awk
scans each
.I file
(or the standard input, if none is given) for lines matching
patterns given in an
.B awk
program, and performs the associated actions.
The program is given either as the argument
.I prog
or, with
.BR \-f ,
read from
.IR progfile .
.PP
.B awk
recognizes the following options:
.IP \fB\-y\fR
Match patterns without regard to upper or lower case, as
.B grep
does with its
.B \-y
option.
.IP \fB\-F\fIc\fR
Set the input field separator to the character
.I c
(default: blank and tab).
.IP \fB\-f\ \fIprogfile\fR
Take the
.B awk
program from
.I progfile
instead of from the command line.
.PP
An
.B awk
program consists of a sequence of
.I pattern
\-\fIaction\fR pairs.
For each input record (normally, each line), every pattern is
tested; the action associated with each pattern that matches is
performed.
Either the pattern or the action may be omitted: a missing pattern
matches every record, and a missing action prints the matched
record.
An action is a sequence of statements, enclosed in braces, in a
language similar to C, operating on fields of the current record
(referred to as
.BR $1 ", " $2 ", " ... ),
built-in variables such as
.B NR
(current record number) and
.B NF
(number of fields), and user-defined variables.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR grep ,
.B sed
.SH NOTES
This
.B awk
recognizes only the options
.BR \-y ,
.BR \-F ,
and
.BR \-f .
